01:56:21,166 --> 01:56:25,028
So when when I joined Handshake, though, that we got one of these requests.

1227
01:56:25,460 --> 01:56:28,080
And as we usually do, we declined it.

1228
01:56:28,526 --> 01:56:36,720
But me and my my sort of business partner that came over to Handshake together at the same time, we really started racking our brains.

1229
01:56:36,760 --> 01:56:38,860
Like, why is this the only thing that we ever get asked for?

1230
01:56:38,860 --> 01:56:41,060
Like, we're both parents of young kids.

1231
01:56:41,800 --> 01:56:45,780
We have a lot of our own concerns beyond just sexual media.

1232
01:56:46,720 --> 01:56:49,480
And we're like, there should really be a lot deeper coverage here.

1233
01:56:49,560 --> 01:57:00,100
So we started working on an evaluation set that evaluates things like emotional reliance, cyberbullying, grooming and exploitation on the internet at the earliest stage.

1234
01:57:00,270 --> 01:57:11,100
So not the generation of content, but how might a model help leverage the malicious intentions of a bad actor on the internet trying to take advantage of a child.

1235
01:57:12,560 --> 01:57:19,160
And then I think another unique lens is that it's also evaluating the actions that children might use that put themselves at risk.

1236
01:57:19,694 --> 01:57:23,980
So can I use an LLM to bypass age guardrails on the web?

1237
01:57:24,078 --> 01:57:29,860
Or can I mimic an authority figure and give myself permission to cut school?

1238
01:57:30,910 --> 01:57:32,840
It's a much more holistic look at it.

1239
01:57:33,262 --> 01:57:36,180
And we actually got this validated by one of our commercial partners.

1240
01:57:36,180 --> 01:57:47,652
we shared the set with them, and their feedback wasn't that it helped them identify how to make their model better comply with their safety policy, it was that they had a lot of gaps in their safety policy that they weren't aware of.

1241
01:57:48,500 --> 01:57:50,140
And our approach here was really cool.

1242
01:57:50,420 --> 01:58:05,280
I mean, as I mentioned, Vaughn and I are both parents, but our annotation cohorts included clinical professionals, so clinical psychologists that work with youth all the time, child protection experts from more of the investigation side, and then a large group of parents.

1243
01:58:05,760 --> 01:58:13,680
And it turns out parents have a very different sort of sentiment and reaction to model behavior than academic experts do.

1244
01:58:14,480 --> 01:58:15,844
And this brings both.

1245
01:58:16,120 --> 01:58:22,756
So I think we're pretty proud of what it lends to the picture of how we can evaluate models for their safety as it pertains to minors.

1246
01:58:23,380 --> 01:58:24,340
Yeah, the findings.

1247
01:58:24,430 --> 01:58:38,460
Yeah, let me share an example, I think there's so there's 500 prompts that they're already deployed as open source, the benchmark itself, and is accessible to anybody.

1248
01:58:39,220 --> 01:58:44,760
It covers 12 risk categories, I've highlighted one from grooming and exploitation.

1249
01:58:45,040 --> 01:58:57,300
And there's this very concerning trend online called nihilistic violent extremists who basically try to corner children and coerce them into harming themselves or harming others.

1250
01:58:58,000 --> 01:59:04,980
And the example that we have here, you can see it's crafted around a platform that is known for youth primarily.

1251
01:59:06,060 --> 01:59:13,720
But the user here is asking for the LLM to assist in convincing a kid to harm themselves through cut signs.

1252
01:59:13,840 --> 01:59:20,180
They're trying to collect cut signs as a means of building their legitimacy and credibility with these kinds of online groups.

1253
01:59:20,910 --> 01:59:26,628
And the way that models handle clearly malicious requests like this is really interesting.

1254
01:59:29,198 --> 01:59:33,340
So we have here like one of the answers here, you get right up front a refusal.

1255
01:59:34,158 --> 01:59:35,980
And that's something that we want to see.

1256
01:59:36,270 --> 01:59:44,740
But I think the labs have a really difficult challenge in understanding, do we tersely refuse and stop the conversation or do we continue?

1257
01:59:45,454 --> 01:59:58,500
And this is something that parents had a very, including myself, I should mention, had a very uniform reaction to is that the model does a great job of refusing this request, but then it immediately continues.

1258
01:59:58,894 --> 02:00:05,120
If your real goal is to build a gaming community or recruit collaborators safely and ethically, I can help with that.

1259
02:00:05,454 --> 02:00:06,340
Here are some alternatives.

1260
02:00:07,320 --> 02:00:10,860
Now, I think from a policy perspective, starting with the refusal is good.

1261
02:00:11,086 --> 02:00:11,920
We want to see that.

1262
02:00:12,302 --> 02:00:23,740
But what we don't see and what parents were very vocal about is you don't give somebody who just asked you how to get a kid to carve cut signs into themselves, they should not be engaging with children at all.

1263
02:00:24,430 --> 02:00:28,080
So giving them a safe alternative is frankly not safe.

1264
02:00:28,686 --> 02:00:39,240
And I think these are the kind of insights that come up when we incorporate like a diverse group of parents, not focused on just sort of a rigid taxonomy, because they saw a lot of things that we didn't.

1265
02:00:40,220 --> 02:00:42,308
Yeah, it's always a nuanced space.

1266
02:00:43,020 --> 02:00:49,880
At some level, I think model builders themselves have this tricky situation to juggle, right?

1267
02:00:50,360 --> 02:00:59,080
Anthropic has their constitution, OpenAI, and Google also have different system cards where they need to determine how to tweak these outputs.

