1563
02:29:40,878 --> 02:29:50,468
So for example, let's say the enterprise is trying to codify the end-to-end mortgage process that we talked about earlier, then that becomes the task.

1564
02:29:50,680 --> 02:29:53,360
And now there are two interesting subparts to it.

1565
02:29:53,460 --> 02:29:55,900
One is that, what models do you use?

1566
02:29:55,980 --> 02:30:01,140
How do you make models, keep them making better as they get deployed?

1567
02:30:01,710 --> 02:30:09,660
And then second is, how do you own that intelligence instead of all that data going out so that you can control it?

1568
02:30:10,158 --> 02:30:16,820
And a big part of what has driven progress in machine learning is codifying those things into benchmark.

1569
02:30:17,678 --> 02:30:24,500
So that a large group of people can look at a number and say, everything that we are doing, we made an update to the prompt.

1570
02:30:25,080 --> 02:30:27,360
We swapped one model with another model.

1571
02:30:27,760 --> 02:30:31,420
Instead of one loop of agent, we did two loops of agent.

1572
02:30:31,640 --> 02:30:34,788
How did it all actually improve the user experience?

1573
02:30:35,438 --> 02:30:47,280
That has to be codified into a way that people can look at some numbers and say, okay, this quantitatively helped move the improved user satisfaction and so on and so forth.

1574
02:30:47,840 --> 02:30:55,020
And Benchmark is a proxy for being able to make a lot of those iterations very quickly in a more scientifically addressed manner.

1575
02:30:55,758 --> 02:31:06,100
That has driven progress of machine learning all this duration, and the first thing enterprises have to sit down and do is think about what does it mean, what does good look like for their use case?

1576
02:31:06,670 --> 02:31:09,680
And more importantly, what does bad look like for their use case?

1577
02:31:09,840 --> 02:31:29,620
So, if it's an end-to-end mortgage automation, circumventing legal processes or well-understood legal norms on how you do pricing of mortgage that Steve and I were talking about earlier is a very important part of partifying into the system, as well as like, how fast can it do it?

1578
02:31:29,700 --> 02:31:34,436
Can it actually like understand all the nuances of my life and give me the best mortgage rates?

1579
02:31:35,118 --> 02:31:40,240
So I think the first thing any enterprise has to do is after defining the task is to build a benchmark.

1580
02:31:40,880 --> 02:31:47,100
And then they start thinking about, should we deploy this as an agent, a closed source agent or with an open source model?

1581
02:31:47,534 --> 02:31:56,260
And if they do deploy it as an open source model, can they come in and use their own data to build their own open source model that they own?

1582
02:31:57,614 --> 02:32:02,948
And it becomes like part of their mode that they can continuously keep improving over a period of time.

1583
02:32:03,630 --> 02:32:09,780
Maybe the last thing I would say here is that models, every model family has very different distribution.

1584
02:32:10,380 --> 02:32:19,780
And one of the more recent learnings in the field of AI has been that if you train the model on its own traces, it learns much better than if you train it on someone else's traces.

1585
02:32:20,020 --> 02:32:20,320
OPD.

1586
02:32:21,020 --> 02:32:21,540
Exactly.

1587
02:32:22,100 --> 02:32:23,520
Which we already did in NotePaperClub.

1588
02:32:23,960 --> 02:32:24,780
On policy.

1589
02:32:25,120 --> 02:32:26,560
On policy distillation, exactly.

1590
02:32:27,022 --> 02:32:41,560
So if an enterprise deploys an open source model or a model that they can take the traces and then improve that model over a period of time on its own traces, then it becomes a compounding flywheel and advantage to them.

1591
02:32:42,158 --> 02:32:47,160
At the same time, fine tuning a model comes with a lot of cost, comes with a lot of complexity.

1592
02:32:47,840 --> 02:32:55,760
